  "query_preview": "..\n\nThe rest of today through Sunday...\n\nA weak upper trough is tracking over the region today. While most\nof the energy from this system remains south of the region, some\nlight snow and flurries may occur over the southern mountains and\nimmediate foothills. \n\nAs the trough moves east, the surface pressure gradient will \ntighten over the west. This may allow for some stronger gap winds\nalong the Livingston to Nye area late tonight into Tuesday. 700 \nmb winds are not expected to be favorable, as speeds will be \naround 30 to 40 kts and the direction will be out of the west to \nnorthwest. 700 mb wind speeds of 45+ kts and a wind direction out \nof the southwest are most favorable for strong gap wind events. \nAs a result, winds are generally expected to remain below 60 mph \n(<10% chance to reach 60 mph), with a 50% chance for gusts greater\nthan 50 mph near Livingston.\n\nThursday into the end of the week, heights fall and Pacific\nmoisture moves into the region. This will lead to lee side \nsurface troughing and favorable surface pressure patterns for \nincreased wind in and near our foothills, especially gap locations\n(Livingston, Big Timber, Harlowton). Increasing mid level winds \n(near 50 knots at 700mb) and tightening surface gradients (15-20mb\nfrom IDA-LWT per the models) suggest a high probability of 50+ \nmph gusts (80-90%) and a good chance of 60+ mph gusts (45-65% \nchance) along the foothills. This may affect holiday travelers in\nhigh profile vehicles. Over the rest of the region, there is \ncurrently a 20-40% chance for gusts greater than 50 mph along the \nDakota border and over the rest of the plains, there is a 50-70% \nchance for gusts greater than 35 mph.\n\nThere is still some uncertainty in the strength of the wave\nThursday into Friday. Currently, the best chance (50-80%) for\nprecipitation remains over the Absaroka/Beartooth mountains,\nalthough there is a low chance (10-30%) for light rain showers\nover the plains. Late Friday into Saturday has a higher chance \n(20-50%) for more widespread rain and snow showers impacting the \nplains, although no snow accumulation is expected outside of the\nfoothills. By Sunday, models are in better agreement for weak\nridging to keep conditions mostly dry outside of the mountains.\n\nHighs will gradually increase from upper 30s to low 40s Tuesday \nto upper 40s to low 50s Friday. Highs then fall back into the 40s\nfor the weekend. Archer",
